174
00:10:02,279 --> 00:10:08,279
Not just our studies for Miami, but our other studies here in Oklahoma and throughout the Southwest.

175
00:10:08,279 --> 00:10:18,279
And that is, we are trying to help utilities such as yours deal with the inevitable cost increases that come with providing utility service.

176
00:10:18,279 --> 00:10:29,279
But it's a sensitive topic everybody wants to talk about and that is increasing rates for electric service or water and wastewater service.

177
00:10:29,279 --> 00:10:32,279
Nobody wants to talk about it because nobody wants to pay more.

178
00:10:32,279 --> 00:10:35,279
Nobody wants to pay more for anything at any time for any reason.

179
00:10:35,279 --> 00:10:40,279
However, you have to keep a couple of very important things in mind.

180
00:10:40,279 --> 00:10:44,279
One is that you try to run your electric utility like a business.

181
00:10:44,279 --> 00:10:50,279
You want the rates that you charge for service to cover all of the costs of providing that service.

182
00:10:50,279 --> 00:10:52,279
Those costs are going up.

183
00:10:52,279 --> 00:10:58,279
And so, therefore, you'll try to, well, you try to charge a rate that recovers all of those costs.

184
00:10:58,279 --> 00:11:03,279
You do have one advantage over many businesses, and that is that you don't have to have to make a profit.

185
00:11:03,279 --> 00:11:06,279
You're just in this to recover your costs.

186
00:11:06,279 --> 00:11:12,279
And so, if your rates don't cover your costs, then, essentially, you're selling a product for a loss.

187
00:11:12,279 --> 00:11:15,309
And that's not good business practice for anybody.

188
00:11:15,309 --> 00:11:24,539
The other thing to keep very much in mind is that it is always a goal of any utility to try to keep rates as low as possible.

189
00:11:24,539 --> 00:11:27,539
Obviously, we want to keep our rates low.

190
00:11:27,539 --> 00:11:42,539
The properly managed utility is that utility that balances the desire to keep rates low with the need to invest in your system to ensure that you provide an acceptable and reliable and consistent quality of service.

191
00:11:42,539 --> 00:11:47,539
Electric systems, like although they're assets, age, they wear out.

192
00:11:47,539 --> 00:11:49,539
They have to be replaced.

193
00:11:49,539 --> 00:11:55,539
You have to invest in them in order to ensure that the system continues to run properly.

194
00:11:55,539 --> 00:11:59,789
And so, ideally, I do work for a utility.

195
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,799
that have incredibly low rates, but they have low rates because they're not putting any money

196
00:12:03,799 --> 00:12:05,299
into their system.

197
00:12:05,299 --> 00:12:10,480
And so not only is the quality of the system declining, but it is also putting your customers

198
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:13,360
at risk of a catastrophic failure.

199
00:12:13,360 --> 00:12:17,240
One of the things that you will see about the rate proposal we're about to present to you tonight

200
00:12:17,240 --> 00:12:23,019
is that it will allow you to do as much as $28 million of investment in the future of

201
00:12:23,019 --> 00:12:24,639
the city of Miami.

202
00:12:24,639 --> 00:12:27,759
And that is another advantage of running an electric utility.

203
00:12:27,759 --> 00:12:32,679
And that is that what the contributions you request from your ratepayers go right back

204
00:12:32,679 --> 00:12:34,679
into the system itself.

205
00:12:34,679 --> 00:12:39,600
And so what they're really doing is investing in the future and ensuring that the system

206
00:12:39,600 --> 00:12:44,440
that you turn over to future generations is a system that is as reliable as the one that

207
00:12:44,440 --> 00:12:45,440
